            <div xml:id="d258">

                <pb facs="0621" n="579" xml:id="pg579"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">No. 258</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>On March 5, Four-Power Exploratory Talks began at Paris in an attempt to agree on
                    an agenda for a possible Foreign Ministers meeting. One of the points of
                    contention concerned the revision of the Treaty of Peace with Italy: would it be
                    discussed at all, and, if so, in what context. For documentation on these talks,
                    see <ref target="frus:frus1951v03p1/pg_1086">volume III, Part 1, pages
                        1086</ref> ff.</p>
                <p>In preparation for these meetings, a policy review paper was drafted by Joseph N.
                    Greene concerning the Treaty of Peace with Italy. This paper, identified as RPTS
                    D–3/10 of March 3, concerned how the United States could counter Soviet
                    allegations of violations of the Peace Treaty. A copy of the paper is in CFM
                    files, lot M–88, box 157, RPTS series.</p>
            </div>
